## Part 1: Introduction to design systems

Every great news page is built on a set of invisible rules. The font choices, the spacing between elements, the way a layout reflows on a phone — none of it is accidental. The best newsrooms codify these rules into what designers call a "design system": a shared vocabulary of colors, type sizes, spacing values and layout patterns that keeps every page consistent, no matter who builds it.

You've already been using one, whether you knew it or not. Our class template's `app.scss` file contains a set of CSS variables — `--color-accent`, `--font-serif`, `--spacing-md` and so on — that your components have been referencing since Week 3. That was a design system. Today, we're going to understand it, improve it, and then learn how to break out of it when a story demands something different.

To see what a mature design system looks like, consider the Reuters Graphics component library we explored in [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-3/reuters-graphics-components.png"
  alt="Reuters Graphics components Storybook showing a library of reusable page elements with consistent typography and color"
  chromeTitle="Reuters Graphics Components"
  chromeUrl="https://reuters-graphics.github.io/graphics-components/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Every component in the library draws from the same set of shared variables — the same typeface, the same spacing scale, the same palette. A headline component from one story looks like it belongs next to a chart from another, because they share a common visual foundation. Change one variable and the entire system updates.

You can see this in action in Reuters' recent visual explainer, ["Mapping the crisis in Iran."](https://www.reuters.com/graphics/IRAN-CRISIS/MAPS/znpnmelervl/) The page is built from the same component library we browsed in Week 3 — the same headline styles, the same caption treatments, the same spacing rhythm — applied to a completely different story. The design system does the work of making it feel like Reuters without any designer having to reinvent the wheel.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-6/reuters-iran-maps.gif"
  alt="Reuters Graphics visual explainer 'Mapping the crisis in Iran' showing maps and charts with consistent Reuters typography, spacing and layout"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The Washington Post, The New York Times, The Pudding and virtually every serious digital newsroom operates this way. The specifics vary, but the principle is universal: define the rules once, apply them everywhere.

Today we'll start by understanding the system that's already in our template. Then we'll find its weak spots and fix them. Finally, we'll learn how to override the defaults when a specific story calls for a different look — the way a Reuters feature about Iran can have its own visual identity while still feeling like Reuters.

